WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.460
Our agent has written useful code to
summarize failed payments.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.900
We need to run it, but we can’t actually
trust it: no human

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:12.260
has reviewed it, and instructions hidden
in its input can

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:13.979
manipulate its behavior.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.500
The first thing we need in order to run
the code securely

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:22.500
is time and memory limits, so faulty
programs can’t run

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.062
indefinitely or exhaust the application’s
resources.

00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:31.127
In terms of access to tools, the code
needs billing access

00:00:31.207 --> 00:00:32.487
in order to run.

00:00:32.807 --> 00:00:36.727
That being said, control must be granular:
permission to

00:00:36.887 --> 00:00:40.487
read payments does not mean permission to
issue refunds,

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:43.847
and permission to refund one customer does
not authorize

00:00:43.927 --> 00:00:45.313
refunds for another.

00:00:46.633 --> 00:00:49.993
These checks and controls have to be
enforced outside the

00:00:50.073 --> 00:00:52.473
model and the generated code.

00:00:52.553 --> 00:00:56.073
Each request must be checked against its
permitted operations

00:00:56.153 --> 00:00:58.754
and scope before it reaches the service.

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:03.907
We also need to see what ran, which
actions were allowed

00:01:03.987 --> 00:01:05.827
or denied, and why.

00:01:06.147 --> 00:01:09.267
Those decisions need to be traceable to
rules that people

00:01:09.347 --> 00:01:11.064
can review and control.

00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:14.127
The summary is complete.

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:16.767
The unauthorized refund is blocked.

00:01:17.087 --> 00:01:21.567
That’s the goal: useful code, running
inside a tightly controlled

00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:23.129
execution environment.
